  "narrative": "I assessed all new discovery evidence against a 10% practically important relative difference, using the absolute population mean of each outcome as the denominator because dependency scores are negative. Signed estimates are already oriented to the claim direction, so positive values support the claim and negative values point away from it.\n\nThe strongest current result is H30: within the feature_034=1 subgroup, outcome_005 is higher/less negative for feature_021=1 versus 0, with signed estimate 0.0294 and 95% interval [0.0037, 0.0551]. The clinical threshold for outcome_005 is approximately 0.0250. The point estimate exceeds this threshold, and the interval is entirely in the claimed direction, but its lower bound is below the practical threshold. Therefore H30 is not acceptable yet, but it is the best candidate for independent validation because it combines a clinically plausible magnitude, direction consistency, and exclusion of the null at 95%. I request validation of H30.\n\nThe corresponding interaction test H29 is imprecise: signed estimate 0.0194 [-0.0185, 0.0573]. It is suggestive but cannot establish that the H14 lead is concentrated in feature_034=1 relative to its complement. H29 is left unresolved but closed because H30 provides a more interpretable direct within-subgroup test.\n\nFor the H13 lead, the subgroup refinement H28 shows almost no within-feature_034=1 association: signed estimate 0.0036 [-0.0444, 0.0516], and the interaction H27 is negative but wide: signed estimate -0.0359 [-0.1054, 0.0336]. These do not establish concentration of the H13 effect in feature_034=1; the H27 point estimate is actually opposite to the claimed direction, although its interval still includes clinically meaningful positive values. Because the intervals remain compatible with clinically meaningful effects in the claimed direction, I keep H27 and H28 unresolved but close them as direct follow-ups. The original H13 population-level association remains unresolved and active, but H30 is a more informative validation target than H13 given its direction-aligned interval and larger relative point estimate.\n\nThe new population-level screens H31-H33 were null or opposite for their pre-specified stronger-dependency claims. H31 has upper bound 0.0243 versus threshold approximately 0.0260; H32 has upper bound 0.0215 versus threshold 0.0255; H33 has upper bound 0.0051 versus threshold 0.0252. In each case, even the direction-aligned confidence bound falls below the 10% clinical threshold, so these are rejected and closed.\n\nThe untested broad screens H15-H19 were also rejected and closed. Their upper practical bounds are below the 10% thresholds, or the point estimates are opposite to the pre-specified direction: H15 upper 0.0169 < 0.0256; H16 upper 0.0068 < 0.0282; H17 upper 0.0213 < 0.0253; H18 upper 0.0231 < 0.0301; H19 upper 0.0319 < 0.0415. None provides support for a clinically meaningful association in its registered direction.\n\nThe overall evidence remains exploratory. No predictor should be treated causally or generalizably at this stage. Feature names are opaque, and the H30 subgroup test was one of several pre-specified refinements, so multiplicity remains relevant. Independent validation of H30 is the most useful next step because a replicated interval that stays above null and ideally above the 0.0250 clinical threshold would materially change the assessment of feature_021 within the feature_034=1 stratum.",
  "research_notes": "Round 2 appraise update:\n- Clinical thresholds used as 0.1 * absolute outcome mean. Key thresholds: outcome_005 ~0.0250, outcome_006 ~0.0175, outcome_008 ~0.0282, outcome_009 ~0.0256, outcome_011 ~0.0301, outcome_001 ~0.0415, outcome_002 ~0.0260, outcome_004 ~0.0255, outcome_010 ~0.0252, outcome_003 ~0.0253.\n- H30 is now the main validation candidate: outcome_005/feature_021 within feature_034=1, direction +1, signed estimate 0.0294 [0.0037, 0.0551]. Point exceeds clinical threshold and excludes null, but lower bound is below threshold. Validate H30.\n- H29 is imprecise interaction and closed unresolved; H30 is more interpretable.\n- H13 remains unresolved/active as the parent population lead, but its feature_034 subgroup refinements are not convincing: H28 is tiny and H27 has opposite point estimate with wide interval.\n- H14 remains unresolved/active as parent; do not accept based on subgroup H30 because H30 is not the population claim and has not independently replicated.\n- Reject and close H15-H19 and H31-H33 because direction-aligned upper practical bounds fall below 10% threshold or observed direction is opposite.\n- If H30 validation comes back with lower bound above ~0.0250 and point estimate stable, accept H30 as a dataset-specific subgroup association. If lower bound remains below threshold but above zero, keep unresolved but probably accept evidence of non-null association and leave clinical magnitude uncertain. If validation reverses or approaches null, close H30 and likely de-prioritize H14.\n- Do not infer causal or generalized biology from opaque predictors; all conclusions are dataset-specific until validation and further context are available."
