ion. They are therefore rejected and closed. The prior H200 result, which excluded null but had a lower bound below the threshold, remains unresolved in substance but closed for active investigation; the new complement and interaction tests do not strengthen it enough to accept.\n\nThe feature_021 screen on other outcomes did not reveal a new clinically meaningful effect. H165, outcome_001, has signed estimate about 0.0006 and interval -0.0396 to 0.0407. Because the outcome_001 threshold is about 0.0415 and the entire interval lies below that threshold, this is reasonably strong evidence against a clinically meaningful feature_021 effect on outcome_001, so I reject it. H166, outcome_008, has signed estimate about 0.0124 with interval -0.0121 to 0.0370, and H167, outcome_011, has signed estimate about 0.0054 with interval -0.0235 to 0.0342. These point estimates are small relative to their thresholds of about 0.0282 and 0.0301, but the upper bounds still cross those thresholds. Rather than claiming absence with false precision, I keep them unresolved and close them because they provide no basis for accepting a clinically meaningful extension of the dominant feature_021 effect beyond outcome_006.\n\nMethodologically, the dominant feature_021/outcome_006 result is so large that multiplicity is unlikely to explain it, but it still cannot be interpreted causally or externally generalized from this dataset alone. The secondary candidates are much more vulnerable to multiplicity, small conditional cells, collider or selection concerns from conditioning on correlated predictor strata, and post-selection bias after many subgroup explorations. I did not see evidence of coding or leakage in these results: cell sizes meet the Welch minimum, predictors are measured variables, and estimates were signed correctly. However, none of the new results meets the stricter requirement that the lower confidence bound support a 10 percent or larger difference. I requested no independent val