cepted signed interaction line H296, together with low-feature_010 simple contrasts and quartile interactions, supports the dataset-specific pattern that the feature_021 association with outcome_006 is very strong when feature_010 is low and strongly attenuated when feature_010 is high. This is effect modification or localization in this supplied dataset only; it does not establish that feature_010 causally modifies the association, nor that the magnitude will generalize. The main feature_021 outcome_006 finding itself has independent validation, but still supports association rather than causality. Negative or unsupported findings are important for narrowing conclusions. The original feature_043 median-interaction claim H272 is not supported after independent validation: discovery suggested a positive interaction but validation R235 had the opposite sign and an adjusted interval spanning no meaningful effect. The feature_057 interaction H261 was also not validated. The feature_002 outcome_006 lines remain unsupported overall and in simple feature_034 strata; validation intervals are near null and do not support a clinically meaningful association. The current H279 result further shows that smaller residual associations involving feature_065 with outcome_015 or outcome_014 should not be promoted without replication. Many other deferred or closed subgroup claims remain unresolved or rejected because the point estimates are small, intervals include null, or the lower bounds do not clear the clinically meaningful threshold. Remaining uncertainty is mainly biological interpretation and external generalizability. Feature_010 modification is data-derived and post hoc, and feature_021's role is inferred from association, not causal mechanism. The most informative next analysis is not broader subgroup mining. If external data become available, pre-specify and test the main feature_021 outcome_006 association and the feature_010 low/high effect modification with multiplicity